{
  "image_name": "1ce8ba9c8b8385661e1314e920864aa2.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how the gut microbiota interacts with the intestinal lining and local immune and signaling systems. At the center is the intestinal mucosa, with bacteria in the lumen above a mucus layer and epithelial cells below. Goblet cells produce mucus, while secreted IgA and antimicrobial peptides help keep commensal bacteria at a controlled distance from the epithelium.\n\nThe side insets highlight four major functions influenced by the microbiota. One shows microbial fermentation of unabsorbed carbohydrates into short-chain fatty acids and gas, emphasizing metabolic activity in the lumen. Another focuses on barrier function, where proteins such as claudins, occludin, ZO proteins, JAM-A, and E-cadherin form tight and adherens junctions that regulate intestinal permeability. A third inset shows enteroendocrine signaling: enterochromaffin cells produce serotonin (5-HT) via TPH1, release it toward sensory fibers, and then reuptake it through SERT, with breakdown to 5-HIAA. The last inset depicts immune activity in the lamina propria, including antigen presentation to T cells, cytokine release, B-cell responses, and antibody production.\n\nOverall, the image presents the gut microbiota as a central regulator of metabolism, epithelial barrier integrity, neuroendocrine signaling, and mucosal immunity.",
